# Where the NAS is, from this Mac. Copy to deploy/deploy.env and set NAS_SSH.
#
# This file is about the NAS: how to reach it, and where things go on it. The
# server's own configuration is not typed again — deploy.sh takes the Firebase
# and Google values from the .env the server runs with on this Mac, and the web
# UI's from frontend/web/.env. deploy/.env is optional, for anything that must
# differ on the NAS (GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI, above all). None is committed.

# Required. Exactly what you type after `ssh`: user@host. A name from
# ~/.ssh/config, a name on the local network, or an address — all fine.
#
# A password-only login is fine too. The script opens **one** connection at the
# start, so ssh asks once and everything after it reuses that. Installing a key
# (`ssh-copy-id user@host`) removes even that one prompt; it is a convenience,
# not a requirement.
NAS_SSH=casa@192.168.50.174

# The photo library on the NAS. Leave it unset the first time and run
# `./deploy/deploy.sh --check`: it will list the shared folders it can see and
# ask you to pick one.
# LIBRARY_PATH=/srv/dev-disk-by-uuid-xxxx/Photos

# Where the compose file, the ledger and the publishing folder live. On OMV,
# put it beside the other services, in the docker-data folder:
#
#     docker-data/MasterPhotoTools/
#         docker-compose.yml   written by the script
#         .env                 written by the script, mode 600
#         data/                the ledger and staging
#         publishing/          publishing draws from here, and empties it
#
# Defaults: REMOTE_DIR=/srv/phototools, and data/ and publishing/ inside it.
# REMOTE_DIR=/srv/dev-disk-by-uuid-xxxx/docker-data/MasterPhotoTools
# DATA_PATH=$REMOTE_DIR/data
# PUBLISHING_PATH=$REMOTE_DIR/publishing

# The port on the NAS. --check refuses if another container already publishes it.
# PORT=3000

# A Docker network to join as well as this service's own — the one a reverse
# proxy (nginx-proxy-manager, say) is on, so it can reach this server by name,
# as http://masterphototools:3000. Unset joins nothing. --check lists the NAS's
# networks if the name is wrong.
# PROXY_NETWORK=

# What the container runs as. Unset means "whoever owns the photographs in the
# library" — on OMV the shared folder is root's, but the files belong to the
# account that copied them in over SMB, and that is who the tools should write
# as. The script refuses to run the container as root. Set these only to
# override that.
# PHOTOTOOLS_UID=1000
# PHOTOTOOLS_GID=100

# The NAS is x86-64, so the build cross-compiles from this ARM Mac. --check
# refuses to go on if this disagrees with what the NAS reports.
# PLATFORM=linux/amd64
